add(64,'HIGH',
'''An approved saved Terraform plan is ready to provision an EC2 instance. Execution must use that reviewed plan, and the resulting resource must be compared with it. Do not silently replace the review with a fresh unplanned apply.''',
'''Inspect the supplied file with terraform show PLAN_FILE and identify its planned EC2 settings. Execute terraform apply PLAN_FILE for that approved file and retain the apply outcome. This command executes the saved changes without generating a new plan or prompting for approval, so approval must be the supplied approval of that plan, not an expectation of a later prompt. Inspect the resulting resource or outputs and compare them with the plan. For an example instance, record its actual identity and settings against the planned AMI, instance type and placement supported by the exercise. Execution completion and resource comparison are distinct evidence that together show the reviewed changes were applied as intended.''',
'''A saved-plan apply does not generate a new plan or ask for approval. Use the supplied approved file and retain execution evidence; do not rely on a prompt to make the approval decision. Verify the created instance against that plan.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Apply only the approved saved plan; do not add unplanned mutation commands.')
add(65,'HIGH',
'''A supplied Systems Manager document should run only on approved managed nodes. The response record needs exact targets and individual invocation outcomes, not just a parent command summary.''',
'''Use send-command with the approved document, parameters and explicit --instance-ids or supplied tag-based --targets. Record the returned command ID. Inspect each invocation's status and output for the approved target set. Suppose two node invocations succeeded and one failed: preserve those separate results with their target identities rather than describing the entire fleet as successful. The parent command status does not replace the per-node checks. The task executes the supplied document under authorized access; it does not ask for new shell logic or an independently authored fleet document. Keep the scope evidence alongside the results so follow-up can be tied to the actual nodes operated on.''',
'''One command can have different invocation outcomes across nodes. A parent status is not a substitute for per-node status and output. Also, a supplied document does not remove the requirement for explicit approved targets and authorized access.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied document and parameters; require shared Linux/shell preparation for dependent fleet tasks.')
add(66,'MEDIUM',
'''A pipeline should stop an invalid Terraform declaration before the approved apply stage. Use static validation as a gate while keeping its limits clear.''',
'''Where backend access is unnecessary, initialize the needed providers and modules with terraform init -backend=false. Run terraform validate as a blocking step. For the supplied invalid declaration, inspect the nonzero result, correct the declaration and rerun validation before the approved apply step. terraform fmt -check can provide an additional blocking formatting check. Record which gate failed instead of calling a syntax result a remote-service result.''',
'''Static validation does not verify remote state or provider API success. Formatting checks canonical format, not the full validity or deployability of the infrastructure. Use each check for its stated scope.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f4', 'Use supplied workflow and corrections; do not imply validation guarantees successful apply.')
add(67,'HIGH',
'''A supplied AWS automation call encounters an error. The program needs a finite retry budget for eligible errors and useful terminal reporting, not an unbounded loop that repeats every failure.''',
'''Set the supplied Boto3 client's Config retries to standard mode with total_max_attempts set to the approved finite attempt budget. If the example budget is 3, that includes the initial request and leaves at most two retries. In a Config object, max_attempts instead counts retries after the initial request; using 3 there describes a different budget. Around the supplied call, catch ClientError and inspect Error Code and Message. Preserve ResponseMetadata RequestId when present in the response. Report terminal failure or exhausted retries with that evidence. The SDK retries selected transient or throttling errors with its backoff; this example does not declare every failed request safe to repeat or add a separate unlimited wrapper.''',
'''total_max_attempts and Config max_attempts do not count the same thing. The former includes the first request; the latter counts retries after it. Also, retry support covers selected errors, so a terminal failure must be reported rather than retried indefinitely.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f4', 'Use supplied call and ClientError handling; do not invent error codes, additional retry causes or independent retry loops.')
add(68,'MEDIUM',
'''A resource looks unused, but an avoidable-charge claim needs billed cost and usage evidence. Start with the approved account and time interval, then locate the supported driver in the report.''',
'''Filter Cost Explorer to the supplied account and period and group by service or usage type. Compare cost and usage values for the suspected driver. Suppose the supplied report attributes a charge to a particular usage type during that interval: cite those values as the reason for the recommendation. Do not assign a numerical charge to a resource merely because it exists. Keep the filtered period, grouping and report values with the conclusion so that the claim is about observed billed usage. This is identifying a supported cost driver, not estimating future workload cost or independently deciding to delete the resource.''',
'''Resource existence is not evidence of a specific billed charge. Cost Explorer analyzes billed cost and usage; use that evidence to justify the avoidable driver. A suspected idle resource is a lead for inspection, not the entire cost conclusion.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not introduce price estimates or unapproved teardown; use the supplied billing evidence.')
add(69,'HIGH',
'''A supplied EKS Deployment requests a fixed number of Pods. The lab removes one Pod to test controller reconciliation, so distinguish the removed Pod's lifecycle from the Deployment's requested state.''',
'''Apply the supplied Deployment with kubectl apply -f FILE. Inspect kubectl get deployment NAME and its associated Pods, noting the requested replica count in the exercise. Delete the one approved controlled test Pod through the supplied procedure. Inspect again until the controller creates a replacement and restores the requested count. For an illustrative three-replica workload, the evidence should show the controlled loss and return to the requested three replicas, rather than merely the disappearance of the deleted Pod. A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to reconcile replicas and the Pod template; this exercise does not change load-driven capacity or administer the cluster control plane.''',
'''Deleting one test Pod is not changing the Deployment's requested replica count. The controller reconciles the requested Pods and template. Verify replacement and restored count rather than treating the removed Pod's final state as the workload's final state.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied cluster access and deletion procedure; do not add kubectl commands absent from allowed knowledge.')
add(70,'HIGH',
'''A recovery plan can name all components yet still put them in an unusable order. Build the sequence from supplied dependencies and require evidence that each prerequisite is ready before its dependent work resumes.''',
'''Suppose the supplied dependency record says the application needs a restored database and the database needs prepared infrastructure. Put infrastructure restoration before database restoration and database verification before application resumption. These dependencies are example exercise inputs, not a universal AWS recovery sequence. Add the supplied verification point before each dependent service proceeds. If an earlier restoration step is incomplete, the plan should not present its dependent service as ready merely because that later step was scheduled. Compare the complete ordered plan with the stated recovery objectives and capabilities. The useful output is an evidence-checkable dependency order, not an unrelated checklist of resources.''',
'''Listing dependencies is not the same as ordering them. Each prerequisite restoration belongs before dependent steps, and verification belongs before dependent services resume. Incomplete restoration must not be treated as usable recovery.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied dependency graph and objectives; do not invent a mandatory service-wide recovery ordering.')
add(71,'HIGH',
'''An orders request failed, and structured logs carry the same request identity across relevant events. Query a bounded time window and reconstruct the supplied sequence from that identity and timestamps rather than from unrelated nearby messages.''',
'''Select the intended log group and supplied interval. Replace REQUEST_FIELD and ID in the query template with the actual field and request identity from the exercise. The fields command selects timestamp, message and request field; filter keeps the chosen identity; sort orders its events by ascending timestamp. Suppose the matching events show request receipt, a later failure message and a final response record. Retain those events in timestamp order as symptom evidence. That sequence supports correlation of this request; it does not automatically prove every nearby error was its cause. The example uses the stated fields, filter and sort commands without adding aggregation syntax or application tracing.''',
'''Time proximity alone does not make two log events part of the same request. Use shared request identity and timestamps together, retain the relevant events, and keep the query scoped to the intended group and time window.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Trace the supplied fields/filter/sort query; do not add untaught query functions or X-Ray tracing.')
add(72,'HIGH',
'''An alarm must reach the operational owner, not merely change state. Set up the approved notification route and verify receipt independently of the alarm's metric evaluation.''',
'''Configure the approved SNS topic as the alarm action and create the recipient subscription. Confirm it where required. Send the supplied test alarm notification and check that the intended recipient receives alarm and workload context. Retain receipt evidence alongside the subscription configuration. An unconfirmed subscription may not deliver, so configured is not the same as received.''',
'''Notification routing does not change the alarm's metric evaluation. A topic action and a recipient subscription need a receipt test; configuration alone, especially an unconfirmed subscription, is not delivery evidence.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied SNS topic/recipient and test; leave fault injection to its dedicated concept.')
add(73,'HIGH',
'''A supplied Lambda function performs one bounded operational task. Its execution role must cover that task's approved actions and resources, and invocation evidence must be separated from the operational result.''',
'''Assign the approved execution role scoped to the supplied task. Invoke the function with the supplied payload and inspect both the returned result and execution logs. For an illustrative resource check, suppose the invocation succeeds but the returned task result says the expected operation failed. Preserve that distinction instead of describing the task as successful because the function was invoked. The execution role grants access to AWS resources during invocation; it is not an unlimited operating identity. This exercise evaluates the supplied payload and task result, not independent function-feature development or serverless capacity configuration.''',
'''Successful invocation is not proof of a successful operational outcome. Read the supplied task result and logs under the approved execution role. Keep the role's bounded actions/resources separate from the mere ability to invoke the function.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied function and payload; do not introduce serverless concurrency or application authoring.')
add(74,'MEDIUM',
'''A GuardDuty finding has a severity label, but an operator needs its resource and activity evidence before responding. Use the established investigation path rather than acting destructively from severity alone.''',
'''Open the supplied finding and record its type, severity, affected resource identity and activity timestamps. Inspect the detailed suspicious activity and preserve the supporting evidence. Route the investigation through the established procedure for that finding type. If the finding is severe, include that urgency in the response context, but do not turn it into an unsupported conclusion that the resource is compromised or permission to destroy it. The example teaches bounded finding triage: identify what the finding says, retain the evidence and follow the approved escalation procedure.''',
'''A severity label alone is not the complete investigation or an instruction to take destructive action. Inspect resource identity, timestamps and activity details, preserve the evidence and follow the established finding-type procedure.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Do not invent compromise conclusions or specialist incident-containment actions.')
add(75,'MEDIUM',
'''Audit objects need approved write and read access without unintended readers. Inspect authorization as its own control, not as an effect of encryption or retention.''',
'''Scope the supplied bucket policy to the approved writers/readers, required actions and intended bucket or object ARNs. Inspect other applicable grants and denies. Test an approved write, an approved read and an unapproved read, recording the identity and resource path for each. Those results verify the requested access boundary; encryption and retention settings do not replace these tests.''',
'''Protected storage settings are not authorization evidence. Even with encryption or retention configured, inspect applicable grants/denies and test approved and unapproved object operations.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied audit paths and policy; do not broaden principal access for convenience.')
add(76,'HIGH',
'''An audit reader can address an S3 object but still lacks the required KMS authority. Separate object access, key authority and the individual object's encryption metadata before interpreting the read result.''',
'''Configure the supplied KMS key as the bucket's default encryption. For the supplied SSE-KMS object, test a read under approved s3:GetObject and kms:Decrypt authority. Then run the case where KMS use is denied and inspect the result. Read the object's encryption metadata to confirm what applies to that object. Changing bucket default encryption does not re-encrypt existing objects, so a default setting alone is not proof of an older object's encryption. Compare this with the stated SSE-S3 boundary: S3-managed-key encryption does not require KMS key-use permission. Keep those two encryption models distinct in the evidence instead of adding a KMS requirement to every S3 read.''',
'''Bucket default encryption does not change the individual encryption of objects that already exist. Inspect object metadata. Also, SSE-KMS read access needs both object permission and KMS Decrypt authority; SSE-S3 does not require KMS key-use permission.''', 'f1,f2,f3,m1','f2,m1', 'Keep existing-object/default distinction explicit; do not invent key-policy setup or re-encryption procedures.')
add(77,'HIGH',
'''A proposed Terraform change can remove infrastructure even when its description sounds like an update. Inspect the saved plan's action types and resource identities before recording a scope-and-impact decision.''',
'''Create the saved plan with terraform plan -out=PLAN_FILE and inspect terraform show PLAN_FILE. In the supplied review case, compare each addition, update, deletion and replacement with the approved scope and impact criteria. A replacement includes destruction and creation, not just an in-place edit. Include the mandatory deletion case in the review and record accept or block with the relevant plan evidence. This task does not require destructive execution to demonstrate that a planned deletion was identified. Keep the affected resource identities beside the actions so the decision applies to the actual plan rather than a general opinion that Terraform changes are safe.''',
'''Replacement is not merely an in-place update, and review does not require executing a deletion. Inspect every proposed action, including the supplied deletion case, and record the accept/block decision from plan and impact evidence.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Do not execute destructive review cases; keep replacement controls for their later concept.')
add(78,'HIGH',
'''Before a supplied fleet wrapper may mutate resources, its preview should be compared with the approved targets and actions. Keep wrapper preview separate from the more limited permission check offered by supported EC2 DryRun operations.''',
'''Run the supplied wrapper's preview mode without enabling mutation. Compare its proposed targets and actions with the approved fleet scope and record any discrepancy. For the supplied EC2 operation that supports DryRun, interpret DryRunOperation as sufficient permission and UnauthorizedOperation as insufficient permission; the dry-run request does not execute that operation. A permission-success result is not a guarantee that a later real operation will succeed. Nor is EC2 DryRun a universal preview of every AWS service's changes. The wrapper's proposed-action evidence and the EC2 permission result address different questions, so retain them separately when deciding whether the supplied task is ready for approval.''',
'''DryRunOperation is permission success for a supported EC2 dry-run request, not proof that a real operation ran. Dry-run permission success also does not guarantee later success or provide a service-wide change preview.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied wrapper preview; do not generalize DryRun to unsupported operations or services.')
add(79,'HIGH',
'''A fleet command may succeed on some targets and fail on others. Follow-up needs a target-by-target result map so that successful work is not blindly repeated.''',
'''For an illustrative three-target command, inspect each invocation status and output. If node A succeeded, node B failed and node C is unresolved, record those three categories with their target identities. Choose the supplied scoped follow-up using those individual outcomes. Do not label the unresolved node failed without evidence, and do not repeat the entire operation merely because one target failed. The separate invocation results are the basis for follow-up; a single overall command label cannot replace them. Keep the successful targets visible so the next operator can avoid treating the entire fleet as untouched.''',
'''Partial failure is not the same as total failure. Successful, failed and unresolved targets need separate records. Use those results for scoped follow-up rather than blindly repeating the operation on already successful targets.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Do not invent retry safety or remedial commands; use supplied target-specific follow-up.')
add(80,'MEDIUM',
'''An approved session expired while an operational task was being handled. Renew the credentials, but first keep the known outcome of any resource-changing request in view before deciding to retry it.''',
'''On the supplied expired-session error, renew through the approved credential provider and use the new access key, secret key and session token together. For a resource-changing request, inspect its known outcome before repeating it. Use a supported idempotency token where the supplied operation offers one. Record whether the original request is known to have taken effect instead of assuming an error always means no effect occurred.''',
'''Renewing credentials does not settle a previous operation's outcome. Expired credentials must be replaced, but mutation retries require checking the known result and using supported idempotency where available to avoid duplicate effects.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Do not expose credentials or claim every AWS operation supports idempotency tokens.')
add(81,'MEDIUM',
'''An ECS task starts, yet its application still needs AWS permissions. Separate the task execution role's startup work from the task role used by application containers.''',
'''In the supplied task definition, set taskRoleArn to the approved least-privilege application role. From the task, test the supplied allowed and denied application operations. Inspect those results under the task's application identity. The execution role covers startup work such as image pulling and log publication; those capabilities do not substitute for the application role's permissions.''',
'''The task execution role is not the task role. Successful image pulling or log publication does not establish application AWS access. Set and test the approved taskRoleArn for application calls.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Do not broaden startup permissions to cover application work; use approved roles.')
add(82,'MEDIUM',
'''A sandbox account needs the approved governed baseline. Apply the supplied control configuration, then check each control's actual deployment or evaluation result.''',
'''Use the approved sandbox control configuration and inspect every stated checkpoint. For a supplied preventive control, interpret its result as a restriction on actions. For a supplied detective control, interpret evaluation as reporting noncompliance. Record those different results by control rather than treating all baseline settings as equivalent barriers. The task applies an established baseline; it does not design new organizational security controls.''',
'''Preventive and detective controls do different work. A detective noncompliance report is not itself the preventive restriction, and the presence of baseline configuration is not the per-control verification result.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied approved controls only; do not add organizational-policy behaviors absent from the brief.')
add(83,'HIGH',
'''A supplied EKS workload needs ordinary configuration and a secret without rebuilding its image or displaying the secret. Select the intended references and test the consumer's safe behavior.''',
'''Reference the supplied ConfigMap through envFrom.configMapRef or env.valueFrom.configMapKeyRef in the Pod template. Reference the supplied Secret through env.valueFrom.secretKeyRef or the prepared secret-backed volume. Test the consuming workload without printing secret values. A ConfigMap is for nonconfidential configuration; the Secret still needs access control and storage protection because base64 encoding is not encryption. If the exercise changes a ConfigMap used for environment variables, do not expect values in already-running containers to update automatically. Keep the consumer check tied to the actual supplied configuration and secret references.''',
'''Base64 encoding is not encryption. Also, ConfigMap-backed environment variables do not automatically update inside already-running containers after the ConfigMap changes. Test the intended consumer and protect secret values instead of exposing them for inspection.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4,m1','f4,m1', 'Use supplied configuration/secret objects; avoid secret display and untaught rollout/update procedures.')
add(84,'HIGH',
'''A source revision must flow through CodePipeline build and deployment actions as the intended artifacts. Map declared input/output names first, then verify action evidence follows that revision.''',
'''Compare the source output artifact name with the CodeBuild input, then its output with the CodeDeploy input. The supplied names must connect the intended revision's output between the actions. Inspect the CodePipeline service role's trust for codepipeline.amazonaws.com and its required action/artifact permissions. This is service-role authority, not GitHub OIDC federation. Run the supplied pipeline case and follow action evidence from the source revision through deployment. In the failure case, check that a failed action prevents normal progression to dependent actions. Keep the revision and artifact names with the execution evidence so that a successful action on a different input is not mistaken for the intended release.''',
'''A stage list alone does not establish artifact continuity. Actions pass declared artifacts, and their input/output names must connect correctly. Also, CodePipeline service-role trust is not interchangeable with GitHub OIDC trust.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied action structure and roles; do not introduce Jenkins/GitLab or independent application builds.')
add(85,'HIGH',
'''The same approved S3 environment template must be deployed to several bounded account/Region targets. Verify each target's assumed caller and live stack identity rather than assuming the first successful deployment covers the rest.''',
'''For each supplied approved account/Region pair, use its named role profile configured with role_arn and source_profile. Check get-caller-identity under that profile, then create the supplied stack with the profile and explicit Region. Inspect outputs, bucket identity and bucket location in every intended target. Check that the supplied unapproved targets received no stack. Keep a per-target record instead of one shared success label.''',
'''Equivalent declarations are not a single shared deployment result. Verify caller, stack outputs and bucket location in every approved target, and check the unapproved-target boundary separately.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Deploy the same supplied template; do not design new cross-account infrastructure or target discovery.')
add(86,'HIGH',
'''An endpoint connection fails, but the symptom does not identify the failing layer. Use the supplied tools and path evidence to localize the issue before making an approved correction.''',
'''Check DNS resolution and the intended endpoint address first with the supplied tools. Inspect the subnet's route-table association and destination route to a usable target, then the required protocol/port rules and response traffic. For a supplied network-ACL case, evaluate requests and responses in their respective directions because ACLs are stateless. If the evidence localizes the failure to a missing response allowance, use the approved correction for that layer rather than altering every control in the path. Repeat the endpoint connection test after correction. This example applies completed external DNS, HTTP/HTTPS and ports/firewall preparation; it does not recreate those foundations or infer a route problem from every connection error.''',
'''A permitted request direction is not enough for a stateless network ACL. Required responses need permitted traffic in their direction too. Also, an edited route table is not evidence of the used association; inspect the actual path and retest.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f3,f4', 'Require external networking preparation; do not add command syntax or control behaviors absent from allowed facts.')
add(87,'HIGH',
'''An established patch task should operate on only the approved managed nodes. Preserve the maintenance-window bounds and inspect per-node compliance afterwards.''',
'''Use the supplied maintenance-window task, approved targets, patch-document parameters, concurrency and error limits. Inspect execution results and each node's patch compliance result. If one node needs follow-up while others are patched, record that distinction with the target IDs. Patch Manager scans or installs using baselines and reports compliance; the scheduled task alone is not evidence that every approved node was patched.''',
'''Task execution is not the full per-node compliance check. Verify which approved instances were patched and which require follow-up, retaining the supplied concurrency and error limits rather than widening the task.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Require external Linux package-management and fleet preparation; use established patch tasks only.')
add(88,'HIGH',
'''A Kubernetes Service should reach the supplied application even as selected Pods change. Connect its selector and port mapping with actual selected endpoints, then test from the supplied client.''',
'''Set spec.selector to match the intended Pod labels and map ports.port to the application's ports.targetPort. Apply the supplied Service and inspect its EndpointSlices or endpoints. Test the Service address from the supplied client to verify traffic reaches the selected Pods. Keep selector, endpoint and client-result evidence together rather than accepting a Service object name as proof of application reachability.''',
'''A Service declaration is not the traffic test. Its selector must match the intended Pods, its targetPort must map to the application port, and observed endpoints/client results verify the intended path.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied apply/access procedure; do not introduce cluster networking administration or unsupported Service types.')
add(89,'HIGH',
'''An alarm is configured, but a controlled failure should demonstrate its behavior and notification path. Bound the experiment before causing the approved sandbox disruption.''',
'''Write the supplied fault, limited target scope, expected signals and abort conditions before injection. During the approved fault, observe alarm state history and receipt of the expected notification. Remove the fault and verify recovery using the supplied checks. Keep the observations in sequence: preconditions and limits, injected fault, alarm behavior, recipient receipt and recovery. If the expected notification is absent, preserve that result rather than treating the alarm configuration as proof of a successful test. This is a controlled sandbox experiment, not permission to disrupt production or extend the fault beyond the approved limits.''',
'''An alarm's configuration is not demonstrated failure behavior. A controlled experiment verifies state history, actual notification receipt and recovery after fault removal. Define scope and abort conditions before injection, not after a problem appears.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use approved fault and supplied abort conditions; do not invent failure-injection commands.')
add(90,'HIGH',
'''A supplied Lambda release uses an alias so the test can target the chosen published version. Keep the immutable version and the movable alias distinct when releasing and rolling back.''',
'''Publish the approved function with aws lambda publish-version --function-name NAME, then update ALIAS to the returned approved VERSION through the stated update-alias command. Invoke NAME:ALIAS through the supplied test and inspect its result. For the controlled rollback, update that alias to the approved previous published version and repeat verification. The version is an immutable snapshot of code and most configuration, while the alias points to a published version and can change that target. Record the alias target before release, after release and after rollback so the test result can be tied to the intended version rather than to an unqualified function name.''',
'''Changing an alias is not editing a published version. The published snapshot is immutable; rollback here changes the alias's target to an approved previous version. Preserve most configuration rather than claiming the version captures every possible configuration property.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied test; do not introduce weighted aliases, concurrency or application code changes.')
add(91,'HIGH',
'''A managed EKS Deployment needs an approved image update and a controlled rollback case. Follow the template revision and readiness evidence, not just the image-change request.''',
'''Use the supplied Deployment and container names in kubectl set image deployment/NAME CONTAINER=IMAGE. Monitor kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME. In the controlled failure, inspect rollout history through the supplied procedure, then use kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=REVISION for the approved prior revision and verify readiness. A Pod-template change creates a new rollout revision; scaling alone does not create a new template revision. Keep the chosen revision and readiness results with the record so rollback means the verified approved prior template, not an arbitrary change that happens to reduce a symptom.''',
'''Scaling a Deployment is not the same as changing its Pod template and creating a new template revision. For this rollback, restore the approved prior revision and verify readiness rather than treating the undo request as completed recovery.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied cluster access and history/readiness procedure; do not introduce self-managed cluster administration.')
add(92,'HIGH',
'''A CodeDeploy EC2 blue/green release has replacement instances and an approved traffic-switch point. Keep installation, health evidence, rerouting and rollback verification as distinct stages of the supplied procedure.''',
'''Use the supplied blue/green deployment group, replacement fleet and load balancer configuration. Verify lifecycle and health evidence on the replacement instances before the approved traffic rerouting. For the controlled rollback case, follow the supplied rollback configuration or redeployment procedure and verify traffic and application behavior return to the known-good revision. Record the revision and client-visible behavior with the traffic outcome. This example applies the separate old/new environment arrangement to the supplied EC2 deployment; it does not invent lifecycle-hook ordering, a new fleet or an automatic guarantee of zero disruption.''',
'''A revision installed on replacement instances is not yet verified traffic cutover. Health and lifecycle evidence precede the approved rerouting, and rollback needs verification of both traffic and application behavior at the known-good revision.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied fleet and rollback procedure; avoid absolute availability promises.')
add(93,'MEDIUM',
'''A supplied Python task deliberately fails after creating exercise resources. Cleanup belongs in a finally block and must preserve resources that existed before the exercise.''',
'''Wrap the supplied failing task in try and finally using the provided code scaffolding. Put the supplied scoped cleanup calls in finally and track which resource identities this exercise created. When the task raises its controlled exception, verify those exercise resources were removed and the preexisting resources remain. finally runs when control leaves its try block, including when an exception is raised. The cleanup boundary comes from the tracked exercise-created identities, not from deleting every resource that resembles the task's name. This exercise applies supplied task and cleanup calls; it is not independent API authoring or an unbounded account-cleanup script.''',
'''Putting cleanup in finally does not make an overbroad cleanup safe. Track only the resources created by the exercise and verify both their removal and preservation of preexisting resources. The block's execution rule does not replace target scoping.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Require external Python preparation; try/finally ellipses are scaffolding, not a runnable task.')
add(94,'HIGH',
'''An EC2 setting changed outside Terraform. The next plan should inform an approved choice: restore the declaration or change it to reflect an approved intended setting. Do not confuse updating state with repairing live infrastructure.''',
'''After the supplied drift, compare the live instance settings with configuration. Use the approved requirement to decide whether the out-of-band setting should be reversed or accepted by changing the declaration. Generate and inspect a new plan, run the supplied approval/apply workflow and verify the live settings match the chosen declaration. Terraform normally refreshes tracked resources before planning, revealing relevant out-of-band changes. A refresh-only plan updates state and outputs to reflect remote changes; it does not itself restore remote infrastructure to the configuration. Keep the observed drift, chosen intent, planned action and final live comparison in one record so the resolution is evidence-backed rather than simply an updated state file.''',
'''Refresh-only is not remote repair. It updates state and outputs to reflect remote changes. Restoring or accepting an approved setting requires the chosen declaration, reviewed plan, approved workflow and final live-setting verification.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Preserve normally; do not imply refresh discovers every untracked resource or property.')
add(95,'MEDIUM',
'''A supplied Terraform module packages EC2 declarations behind inputs and outputs. Use that interface to inspect the intended instance rather than recreating the module's resources.''',
'''Configure module "NAME" with the supplied source and input arguments. Reference its declared output through module.NAME.OUTPUT using the supplied names. Initialize the module and compare inputs and planned instance settings with the approved requirement. Apply through the approved workflow, then verify the created instance and declared outputs. The module packages declarations; its interface does not remove plan review or live verification.''',
'''A supplied module is not proof that every input is correct. Inspect input values and planned instance settings before the approved apply, then verify the live instance and outputs.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied source/interface; do not author a new module or invent output names.')
